omponent-part=3D"accordion-content"><span data-as=3D"p">LangGraph executes =
nodes within <a class=3D"link" href=3D"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/pytho=
n/langgraph/graph-api#graphs">supersteps</a>, meaning that while parallel b=
ranches are executed in parallel, the entire superstep is <strong>transacti=
onal</strong>. If any of these branches raises an exception, <strong>none</=
strong> of the updates are applied to the state (the entire superstep error=
s).</span><span data-as=3D"p">Importantly, when using a <a class=3D"link" h=
ref=3D"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence">checkpo=
inter</a>, results from successful nodes within a superstep are saved, and =
don=E2=80=99t repeat when resumed.</span><span data-as=3D"p">If you have er=
ror-prone (perhaps want to handle flakey API calls), LangGraph provides two=
 ways to address this:</span><ol>
<li>You can write regular python code within your node to catch and handle =
exceptions.</li>
<li>You can set a <strong><a href=3D"https://langchain-ai.github.io/langgra=
ph/reference/types/#langgraph.types.RetryPolicy" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"n=
oreferrer" class=3D"link">retry_policy</a></strong> to direct the graph to =
retry nodes that raise certain types of exceptions. Only failing branches a=
re retried, so you needn=E2=80=99t worry about performing redundant work.</=
li>
</ol><span data-as=3D"p">Together, these let you perform parallel execution=
 and fully control exception handling.</span></div></details>
